by NewtTheWolf at 2026-07-31T18:19:25Z

Thanks for the merge prep @nicolas-grekas. I checked the two open questions live on a real account.

**Authentication.** The transport uses `consumerKey` / `consumerSecret` request
headers, which is the current documented method for `/mail/send`, confirmed with a
live send (HTTP 200 + `mid`). The `authuser` / `authpass` you saw is the older
method: the "Send email V2" page at https://serversmtp.com/turbo-api-1/#send-email-v2
still documents it, while the current OpenAPI reference at
https://serversmtp.com/turbo-api/ documents `consumerKey` / `consumerSecret`. They
also aren't SMTP-only: the same pair authenticates the SMTP relay (`AUTH` returns
`235`, message queued) and the API (200).

**`from` as `"Name" <address>`.** Accepted, and delivered with the name intact. It's
a standard [RFC 5322](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5322#section-3.4) mailbox, not
a provider field. The bridge doesn't hand-roll it: `from` is
`$envelope->getSender()->toString()`, and Symfony's `Mime\Address::toString()` always
quotes the display name, so `"Spitzli, Dominik" <addr>` can't split the address. I
sent bare, quoted, comma and inner-quote forms live and all were accepted.

I also confirmed the attachment fix end-to-end (a `.txt` and a `.png`, both delivered),
and pushed a small follow-up for inline images. `$email->embed()` parts arrived as
plain attachments, because TurboSMTP keys inline parts by `<cid@sender-domain>` and a
bare `cid:logo` reference never matches. `getAttachments` now sets `content_id` for
inline parts and `getPayload` qualifies the HTML `cid:` with the sender domain.
Verified live (renders inline in Proton and Thunderbird) and covered by a unit test.

